{"title": "CSCI-101: Principles of Computing - Lesson Blueprint", "learningObjectives": ["Students will define and explain the core concepts of computing, including the relationship between hardware, software, and data.", "Students will demonstrate the ability to break down a problem into sequential, executable steps using pseudocode or a programming language.", "Students will articulate how computing impacts daily life and the world, and practice oral and written communication through structured assignments."], "beats": [{"title": "Lesson Introduction and Course Scope", "claim": "CSCI-101 introduces the central ideas of computing and demonstrates how computing changes the world and impacts daily lives.", "example": "Students will examine a short case study of how algorithmic sorting algorithms optimize data processing in modern search engines.", "visualDirection": "Display a slide with the course title, a brief overview of the syllabus, and a graphic showing computing's impact on daily life (e.g., healthcare, transportation, entertainment).", "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-101"]}, {"title": "Hardware-Software-Data Relationship", "claim": "Computing relies on the interaction between hardware, software, and data to process information and produce outputs.", "example": "Students will trace how a simple calculator app uses hardware (CPU, memory), software (code), and data (numbers) to perform an addition operation.", "visualDirection": "Show a diagram with three interconnected circles labeled Hardware, Software, and Data, with arrows indicating the flow of information between them.", "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-101"]}, {"title": "Problem Decomposition and Sequential Steps", "claim": "Students will develop step-by-step written solutions to basic problems by breaking them into manageable, sequential tasks.", "example": "Students will write a pseudocode sequence for a 'find the maximum number in a list' problem, listing each comparison and update step explicitly.", "visualDirection": "Provide a template slide with numbered steps for pseudocode, highlighting the 'input', 'process', and 'output' sections.", "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-101"]}, {"title": "Introduction to Programming Language Basics", "claim": "Students will implement their solutions using a programming language, focusing on syntax, variables, and control flow.", "example": "Students will write a Python script that declares variables, uses a loop to iterate through a list, and prints the maximum value.", "visualDirection": "Display a code editor window with syntax highlighting, showing variable declarations, a for-loop, and a print statement.", "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]}, {"title": "Data Types and Variables", "claim": "Python objects have identity, a type, and a value, and names are bound to objects within namespaces and scopes.", "example": "Students will compare integer, float, and string variables, explaining how each type affects data storage and operations.", "visualDirection": "Show a table comparing variable types (int, float, str), their memory representation, and example assignments.", "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]}, {"title": "Control Flow and Conditionals", "claim": "Statements control execution and bindings, while exceptions interrupt normal control flow until a matching handler processes them or they propagate.", "example": "Students will implement an if-else statement to check if a user input is positive, negative, or zero, and handle invalid input with a try-except block.", "visualDirection": "Display a flowchart showing the decision path for an if-else condition, with arrows branching to true/false outcomes.", "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]}, {"title": "Functions and Abstraction", "claim": "Functions and classes organize code into reusable components, reducing the number of interacting concerns through decomposition and abstraction.", "example": "Students will define a function that calculates the average of a list of numbers, then call it multiple times in the main program.", "visualDirection": "Show a code snippet with a function definition and two separate calls, highlighting the reusable block.", "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]}, {"title": "Debugging and Testing", "claim": "Debugging separates syntax, runtime, and logical failures, while decomposition and abstraction reduce the number of interacting concerns.", "example": "Students will run a test case with incorrect input, identify the error type, and fix the code to handle the edge case correctly.", "visualDirection": "Display a split screen: left side shows the error message, right side shows the corrected code with comments explaining the fix.", "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]}, {"title": "Communication and Collaboration", "claim": "Students will demonstrate oral and written communication skills through assignments such as short papers, homeworks, group discussions, and debates.", "example": "Students will present their debugging process in a 2-minute group discussion, explaining the error, the fix, and the lesson learned.", "visualDirection": "Provide a slide with bullet points for a group discussion rubric, including sections for 'Problem', 'Solution', and 'Reflection'.", "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-101"]}, {"title": "Practical Analysis Checklist", "claim": "Students will apply durable concepts and problem-solving methods to evaluate their own and peers' code for correctness, readability, and adherence to course standards.", "example": "Students will use a checklist to review a peer's Python script, verifying variable types, control flow logic, and error handling.", "visualDirection": "Display a printable checklist with boxes to check off: 'Variables declared correctly', 'Control flow matches problem', 'Error handling included', 'Code is readable and commented'.", "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-101", "python-language-reference"]}
                    ]
                    }
